+      "kind": "concept",
+      "title": "Isotropic simplex: localized admissible Neumann modes attain the sharp directional bound",
+      "statement": "DRAFT proof-method concept; this special-case argument was independently checked by w355 and assigned referee w356. No novelty or full KLS claim. Let d>=2, K=(d+1)(d+2), X be uniform on Delta_d={x_i>=0,sum_i x_i<=1}, m=(1,...,1)/(d+1), Sigma=(I-J/(d+1))/K with J the all-ones matrix, A=Sigma^(-1/2), Y=A(X-m), a=sqrt(2K), and v=Ae_1/a. Put Z=Proj_(v-perp)Y, T=<v,Y> and S=sum_(j=2)^d X_j. Given 0<epsilon<1, choose a nonzero smooth eta supported in (epsilon/4,epsilon/2) and put f(Y)=eta(S) sin(pi T/(a(1-S))). Each such f has finite full energy, E[f|Z]=0, E Var(f|Z)=E eta(S)^2/2 and E|partial_v f|^2=(pi^2/(2a^2)) E[eta(S)^2/(1-S)^2]. Its directional quotient is at least (2K/pi^2)(1-epsilon)^2, and the support has positive probability. Letting epsilon go to zero proves that the optimal directional conditional coefficient is 2K/pi^2. This card records the localized admissible witness method, rather than duplicating the common simplex-constants card. It does not bound the full-gradient KLS constant.",
