+      "logical_name": "isotropic-simplex-polynomial-directional-and-full-gradient-energy-family",
+      "kind": "concept",
+      "title": "Isotropic simplex: exact directional and full energies of the polynomial family",
+      "statement": "DRAFT independently checked special-case formula: w355's full-packet audit checked Section 5; w356 kept its d=2,k=2 specialization. No novelty or full KLS claim. Let d>=2, K=(d+1)(d+2), X be uniform on Delta_d={x_i>=0,sum_i x_i<=1}, m=(1,...,1)/(d+1), Sigma=(I-J/(d+1))/K with J the all-ones matrix, A=Sigma^(-1/2), Y=A(X-m), a=sqrt(2K), and v=Ae_1/a. Put Z=Proj_(v-perp)Y, T=<v,Y> and S=sum_(j=2)^d X_j. For every integer k>=1 put t=1-S, f(Y)=T t^k and M_p=(p+1)! d!/(d+p)!. Then E[f|Z]=0, E Var(f|Z)=(K/6) M_(2k+2), E|partial_v f|^2=M_(2k), and E|grad f|^2=[1+k^2(d-1)/(3(d+1))] M_(2k). Thus the exact directional quotient is R_(d,k)=(K/6)(2k+2)(2k+3)/[(d+2k+1)(d+2k+2)], while the full-gradient quotient is R_(d,k)/[1+k^2(d-1)/(3(d+1))]. The extra factor is the transverse gradient cost: grad_Y S is perpendicular to v and has squared norm 2(d-1)/((d+1)K). For d=2,k=2 these give variance 1/2, directional energy 1/3 and full energy 13/27. The family generalizes the separately submitted triangle witness and explicitly preserves all gradient terms.",
